<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Farighunid dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">900 CE – 1010 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Rulers of Guzgan in northern Afghanistan who patronised the Hudud al-Alam, the oldest surviving geography written in Persian.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Farighunids were an Iranian dynasty ruling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guzgan">Guzgan</a> (Juzjan), the region between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Balkh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merv">Merv</a>, from at least the late 9th century. They acknowledged the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/samanid-empire">Samanid Empire</a> as overlords and were among the most prominent local princes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khurasan</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Farighunids were known as patrons of learning. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudud-al-alam">Hudud al-Alam</a>, an anonymous geography of the known world completed in 982, was dedicated to the Farighunid amir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abul-harith-muhammad">Abu'l-Harith Muhammad</a>. They kept a court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guzgan">Guzgan</a> and married into the family of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavid</a> ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabuktigin">Sabuktigin</a>. Their capital is uncertain, often placed at Anbar or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sheberghan">Shiburghan</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Farighunids transferred their loyalty from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/samanid-empire">Samanids</a> to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavid Empire</a>. After the death of the last amir around 1010, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni">Mahmud of Ghazni</a> absorbed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guzgan">Guzgan</a> into his realm.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="emirate, 900 CE – 1010 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">emirate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central Asia and the Steppe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:77.61%;width:2.19%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>900 CE – 1010 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Abu'l-Harith Muhammad</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Persian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Farighunids, Al-e Farighun</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=955&amp;focus=farighunid-dynasty" aria-label="Farighunid dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6373 940 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Farighunid dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6588 1010h0M6748 1217h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6693 1196h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M6693 1196h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Afghanistan. <a href="/atlas?year=955&amp;focus=farighunid-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

